# Lovelace Intermediate Representation (LIR)

Crate `lovelace_lir` in `lir/` is a **static** library. It owns:

- In-memory LIR types (modules, subroutines, signatures, instructions, value types)
- Builders and validators for those types
- Versioned binary (`.lir`) and textual (`.tlir`) codecs (encode/decode/print as implemented)
- Analysis and optimization passes **when asked** (none until then)

`lovelace_lir` may depend on `lovelace_common` only. Do not emit WASM, WIT, or WAT from this crate.

## Machine model

- Stack machine with sequential (linear) byte memory, in the spirit of WASM core.
- The operand stack is implicit at run time; it is not stored in the module.
- Opcodes are **Lovelace** 16-bit codes (little-endian `u16` in the stream), **not** WASM opcode bytes. The compiler backend lowers LIR to WASM later.
- Instructions may have immediates after the 2-byte opcode; length varies by opcode. Document `Encoded_Length` for the stream size.

## Value types (closed set)

- **`unit`** — no stack value
- Signed: `i8` `i16` `i32` `i64` `i128`
- Unsigned: `u8` `u16` `u32` `u64` `u128`
- Floating: `f16` `f32` `f64`

**Do not introduce a `void` type.** LIR has no `void`. Procedures are subroutines whose return type is **`unit`**.

Every subroutine **must** have a return type. Returning `unit` will instruct the Lovelace backend that the subroutine is a **procedure** and pushes nothing on the operand stack. Non-`unit` return types push one value of that type.

No references or aggregates in the closed set until specified. `I128`, `U128`, and `F16` are first-class in LIR; mapping them (and `unit`) to WASM is a backend problem.

## Modules and subroutines

- A module has a UTF-8 **name**, a `u32` **flags** bitset (not string tags), dependencies on other LIR modules (by name), and a list of subroutines.
- Call metadata bits **flags**, not tags.
- Each subroutine has a **signature** (name, **mandatory** return type, zero or more unnamed parameter types), **flags** (`export`, `entrypoint` as flag bits), and an instruction sequence (may be empty).
- Only opcode required until more are specified: `No_Operation` / `noop`.

## Formats

- **`.lir`** — versioned binary; magic `LIR\0` (`4C 49 52 00`); length-prefixed UTF-8 strings; little-endian fixed-width integers (not LEB128). After module metadata, a **subroutine preamble** lists each subroutine name with an absolute 0-based file offset to its record (for future import scanning). Not present in `.tlir`.
- **`.tlir`** — versioned WAT-inspired S-expression text; writers/printers as implemented; do not invent a `.tlir` parser until asked.

See [docs/lir.md](../../docs/lir.md), [docs/lir-binary.md](../../docs/lir-binary.md), and [docs/lir-text.md](../../docs/lir-text.md) for the normative layout.
